A1. Doughboys. ~ MOGULS
A7. What a dry becomes when he reaches the utmost degree of dryness. ~ DRIEST
A13. This was a pretty shady affair at Palm Beach. ~ PARASOL
A14. What over-zealous husbands are always in. ~ EARNEST
A16. This's reported as being over. (Hope it's the depression.) ~ ON
A17. This character often breaks into the Greek tabloids. ~ BETA
A18. Fished. ~ LIED
A19. What to say when words fail you. ~ AH
A20. Feminine, and very, very personal. ~ SHE
A22. The lad who got there. ~ SUCCESS
A24. This's how the triangle started. ~ TRI
A25. To carry, as a non-Volstead load. ~ TOTE
A27. If you attempt to do this and your act falls down you'll be sitting on a cold, cold world. ~ SKATE
A28. A beastly couch. ~ LAIR
A29. A pretty girl shipwrecked in a deserted island would be this, wouldn't she? ~ ALONE
A31. She really hadn't a thing to wear, poor thing. ~ EVE
A32. These were our youthful years. ~ TEENS
A33. Now and then you'll find a man who does this with interest. ~ LENDS
A34. One of the skin troubles the Pilgrims had to contend with. ~ RED
A35. A brilliant show (originally it was first seen in France). ~ ECLAT
A36. What the professor's rolling cubes are made of. ~ OS
A37. A preposition. ~ AT
A38. A swell affair that turned into an uproar. ~ OPERA
A41. Racketeering or no racketeering, this always stands for the Chicago Chamber of Commerce. ~ CCC
A44. One of the sweeter things of life. ~ SUGAR
A48. This'll strike you numb. ~ PALSY
A49. This State is second to none. (Florida and California, please note.) (Abbr.) ~ ALA
A50. What baseball players do when they strike out. ~ ERASE
A51. Something you can do in prison with impunity. ~ TIME
A52. City slickers' manners. ~ SUAVE
A54. This has banks in Canada. ~ ERIE
A55. The favored few. ~ INS
A56. These can be had for the asking. ~ ANSWERS
A58. This's a perfect blotter. ~ INK
A59. A preposition. ~ OF
A60. This's rather bitter to swallow. ~ ALOE
A61. A human moo. ~ MOAN
A63. Insane Experiment. ~ IE
A64. Where to find a girl of retiring habits. ~ NUNNERY
A66. What a kind word from your mother-in-law will do for you. ~ ASTOUND
A68. The ties that bind men to their homes. ~ LEASES
A69. Here's a pointer on the steel market. ~ NEEDLE

D1. One of the pitfalls of Broadway. ~ MANHOLE
D2. An old connection. ~ OR
D3. A good gift for a salesman. ~ GAB
D4. What an employer does in these hard times. ~ USES
D5. An indolent sort of eater (neither chorus girls nor the kind you'd see in the best restaurants). ~ LOTUS
D6. The guy who fights it out sensibly at home. ~ SLACKER
D7. This's as good as struck out (no runs, one error). ~ DELETED
D8. What Father expected to get last Christmas, and didn't. ~ RAISE
D9. Passions that make men mad. ~ IRES
D10. This, too, is a logical conclusion. ~ END
D11. Sad experiment. ~ SE
D12. The Tsar's partner during the war. ~ TSARINA
D13. The kind of cards you get from Paris. ~ POSTAL
D15. This's driven many a man to drink. ~ THIRST
D21. Where English boys'll do their stuff. ~ ETON
D23. This was mortgaged in the early days. ~ CAVE
D24. The dough that's made in China ! ~ TAEL
D26. What you do when you take a check to the bank. ~ ENDORSE
D28. This room is used by college boys for sleeping purposes. ~ LECTURE
D30. This's rather trying. ~ ESSAY
D32. What to do in order to turn a tame cat into a wild, wild one. ~ TEASE
D38. Here we have another alternative. ~ OPTION
D39. The kind of situation nobody wants. ~ PAINFUL
D40. After a seedy start these went into shady business. ~ ELMS
D41. Obsolete highways. ~ CAUSEYS
D42. The iron hand. ~ CLAW
D43. An early clubman. ~ CAVEMAN
D45. This's really what gave Garibaldi his start. ~ GARI
D46. This's ever so silly. ~ ASININE
D47. What garlic did last year all over Italy. ~ REEKED
D52. What night watchmen do when they fall asleep. ~ SNORE
D53. Worn away. ~ EROSE
D56. These were much put down in England. ~ ALES
